On Exam 1B,

Problem (1abc) is a very simple but tedious problem similar to one that has=
 been worked out. Please do it very carefully so that you get 17 points. Do=
 not lose patience. All it requires is patience. Al the parts will match if=
 you pay close attention to all the details.

Problem (2a) is also very simple..  Please refer to the corresponding probl=
em in the Solved Exam and record the work exactly as it is done there ON A =
SEPARATE PAGE. DO NOT SQUISH ALL THE WORK ON THE PAGE CONTAINING THE PROBLE=
M.

Problem (3a) is also a an easy problem. Please refer to the corresponding p=
roblem in the folder entitled Solved Exam and work it out sllowly and caref=
ully.

Problem (2b) is similar to (2a) and requires some thought. It is not diffic=
ult but it does test your attention to detail. Please pay close attention. =
Follow the organisation in the Solved Exam.

Problem (3b) tests your understanding of Equivalence Relations.  DO NOT WRI=
TE NONSENSE DOWN. If TRUE PROVE IN GENERAL BY PALYING A PROOF GAME WITHOUT =
ASSUMING ANYTHING ABOUT P AND Q. IF FALSE FIND A WITNESSES P AND Q TO THE F=
ALSEHOOD OF THE STATEMENT. SUCH A WITNESS IS CALLED COUNTEREXAMPLE.

Problems (2c)  tests your understanding of functions and partial orders and=
 how they interact.

Problem (3c) is really straightforward but may be time-consuming depending =
on how many elements your set has. It asks you to investigate the relations=
hip between equivalence relations and partitions for a 'small' set. I stron=
gly recommend looking at Wikipedia articles on Equivalence Relations and on=
 Partitions both of which have very nice pictures to go with the text.

[https://du11hjcvx0uqb.cloudfront.net/dist/images/email_signature-d2c588061=
